Z||000002||Greg Piet||03-08-2001||07:58 PM||||I dont want to post how to hotwire your car, as someone could use that to harm another member's car or possibly mine.<P>Since most people can break a window Ill mention to you how I got into my car without damage to the car.<P>I used a piece of 1/8 to 1/4 inch tubing..<BR>put an L at the end about 1 inch long<BR>slide it in through the side window...  press the unlock key, or try to manually slide the unlock mechanism.<P>I have also seen on this thread if you give a really hard whack on the roof just behind the sunroof glass latch, the latch will pop open and you can get in then through there by using a coathanger to open the final part of the latch.||||reg||1
Z||000003||KRMFiero||03-08-2001||09:32 PM||||hmmm if you wanna hot wire it just buy one of them remote control car starters from K-Mart and just cross some of the wires it tells you took splice into, I'm sure you'll figure it out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||JBGehling||03-08-2001||09:45 PM||||Um... can't you just call a locksmith?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hank is Here||03-08-2001||11:03 PM||||Well breaking a window is actually hard, at least for me.  Have you ever tired to break a car window?  In my family we have a small twonig company, and evey now and again I play with the wrecks, Usually I cannot break a window when I try but evey now and again I do .  <BR>To get into the car a wedge at the top of the window would work the best then insert a coathanger and hit the locks.  <P>To hotwire well, If you break off the left hand side cover of the steering colum then break the wheel lock and push the trigger forward you have hotwired it, but you also destory the steering colum.   <BR>Gee I have fun playing with wrecks some days  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||SteveJ||03-09-2001||09:53 AM||||I find this thread of breaking into our cars counterproductive to our best interests.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Greg Piet||03-09-2001||11:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SteveJ:<BR><B>I find this thread of breaking into our cars counterproductive to our best interests.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Possibly one day when you lock your keys in the car with your headlights on, or your baby in the car or with the engine running at a lonely rest stop, this thread may prove to be very productive, for now you know what tree branch you need to find...  crank out your pocket knife and you have a nice smooth stick, and a prying tool.<P>A previous thread on this subject is what allowed me to get into my car when by some brain lapse I locked both sets of keys in the car with the dome light on.<P>Finally, I appreciate your view on this subject, but I must wholeheartedly disagree with your assesment of the usefulness of this  topic.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Standard||03-09-2001||11:27 AM||||Well, the fact of the matter is that our cars have been around for about 15 years, and any tricks a thief might need are already out there.  They're not the most secure cars in the first place (as anybody who has a car with frameless windows will tell you) and if somebody really wants it they're going to get it.  All they need is a rock to break the window... of course, if you did get your car stolen wouldn't you be a little happier if it came back without any broken windows?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
